>Well, I haven't been paying enough attention to your new
>distinctions between "spell" and "magic" resistance to know
>where these fall now, but I could generally care less about magic
>missles-

Like I said earlier, Angels like to cast magic missile every turn.
These missile spells tend to do about 9d6 damage per hit. Do you
_really_ want them to be constantly chipping away at your hit points at
such a crucial time?

>If you consider that a big difference, then your path is also
>changing the fundemental meaning of hit points- which in measure
>how much damage you take from something.

www.d20srd.org says that "Hit points mean two things in the game world:
the ability to take physical punishment and keep going, and the ability
to turn a serious blow into a less serious one."

The first meaning seems to fit your definition, and the second meaning
seems to fit mine.  My conception of half disintegration is that the
seriousness of the effect it has on the character cannot ever be
reduced.

Certainly, weaker characters are much less able to "keep going" after
getting half-disintegrated than stronger characters are. This is
reflected in the fact that, after being half-disintegrated, high-HP
characters have more _remaining_ hit points than low-HP characters.

I choose to believe that my reasoning is sound.



